{"date": "2026-10-24", "title": "spill-order fix via guard-in-caller (00458a00 exact)", "text": "00458a00 (134B, CRobloxReportPaneView) was stuck at 98%% across 116 flag/compiler sweeps; the only real residue was a 6-byte instruction swap: target has 'mov eax, esp' BEFORE the EH arg spill 'mov [esp+0xc], esp', the stored source produced the reverse order. Fix: spell the dance as a guard in the caller with two call sites sharing one by-value arg-area temp, copying exact sibling 004589c0: if (source) forward(source->get()); else forward(Result()); where get() is an sret thiscall member (matches 0x403040, ret 4) and the two forward(...) calls tail-merge; because BOTH branches write the same temp, its address is materialized (mov eax, esp) before the spill, reproducing target order. Pinned roc-cl 21022 with /O2 /GS- /EHsc /MD. Also learned: when the candidate extern data symbol is unresolved, roc check's diff renders the disp32 placeholder as a literal like 'mov edx, dword ptr [4]' even though the site is a masked DIR32 reloc - verify with a COFF object dump (match.coff_functions + relocs) before chasing it as a data mismatch; it was cosmetic here. Reusable for any near-miss whose residue is only an EH-spill vs address-materialization swap around a branched by-value argument construction."}
